# Line endings are decided here and not by each clone's configuration. Two
# toolchains across three operating systems is the setting in which a diff fills
# with line ending noise, and a contributor whose clone happens to have
# core.autocrlf set one way produces a diff nobody else can read.
#
# eol=lf rather than eol=native, in the index and in the working tree both. A
# working tree that differs from the index by line ending is where the confusion
# starts: a tool that reads a file from disk and one that reads it from git see
# different bytes, and neither of them is wrong.
#
# What the tree holds is printed rather than restated here:
#
#     git ls-files | sed 's|.*/||' | sed -n 's/.*\(\.[A-Za-z0-9]*\)$/\1/p' | sort -u
#
# and how git is reading each tracked file after these rules apply:
#
#     git ls-files --eol

# The default. text=auto leaves git to detect a binary file and to leave it
# alone, so a future image or font is not normalised into corruption by this
# line. Everything git reads as text is LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The text types in the tree today, declared rather than detected. text is not
# text=auto here, because detection reading one of these as binary would silence
# the rule for that file without saying so.
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.lock text eol=lf
*.rs   text eol=lf
*.ts   text eol=lf

# The tracked files with no extension, anchored to the root so a later file of
# the same name somewhere else does not inherit a rule aimed at these. /build is
# a shell script, where a carriage return is not cosmetic: it reaches the
# interpreter as part of the last word on the line.
/DCO     text eol=lf
/LICENSE text eol=lf
/build   text eol=lf
/.nvmrc  text eol=lf

# The same, for the file that says what the client formatter skips. It has no
# extension either, and it is read line by line by a program that would take a
# carriage return as part of the pattern on the line.
/client/.prettierignore text eol=lf

# The other scripts, declared for the same reason as /build: a carriage return
# in a shell script is not cosmetic, it reaches the interpreter as part of the
# last word on the line. Each one is anchored to the directory it sits in, so a
# later file of the same name elsewhere does not inherit a rule aimed at these.
/client/escape-scan       text eol=lf
/client/prove-gates       text eol=lf
/client/suppression-scan  text eol=lf
/text-scan                text eol=lf
/pr-body-scan             text eol=lf
/prove-pr-body            text eol=lf
/regenerate               text eol=lf
/prove-determinism        text eol=lf
/format                   text eol=lf
/lint                     text eol=lf
/prove-quality            text eol=lf
/test                     text eol=lf
/test-needs-a-database    text eol=lf
/test-scan                text eol=lf
/test-sealed              text eol=lf
/prove-headless           text eol=lf
/prove-sealed             text eol=lf
/coverage                 text eol=lf
/licence-scan             text eol=lf
/licence-apply            text eol=lf
/prove-licences           text eol=lf
/prove-lock-files         text eol=lf
/workflow-scan            text eol=lf
/prove-workflow-scan      text eol=lf

# Not a script, and declared for the same reason all the same. Every line in it
# is one entry or one entry's reason, read line by line by a program, and a
# carriage return would arrive as part of the licence identifier on the line.
/licences-allowed         text eol=lf

# When the first image or font arrives, declare it with git's own `binary`
# macro rather than with `-text` alone. The macro unsets `text`, which the
# encoding rule reads, and `diff`, which is what makes the line ending rule pass
# the file over. Declaring only one of the two leaves the file judged by the
# other rule, and the refusal that follows is a real one whose message points at
# the wrong thing. There is no such file in the tree today, so this paragraph is
# guidance and not a rule, which is why nothing below it declares one.
